{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794311067,"nonce":"gQX+3COzo6iGk+ITBQFsk7pOlOs=","title":"The \"Waiting for an organization\" panel on `/orgs/_new` (PR #148) cannot show a","message":"GitHub connection's account login or account id. `ConnectionDto` carries\nneither, and the account login is not stored at all: the connection record\nhas only `account_id` and `installation_id`.\n\nToday the UI reads the installation id out of the `github-install-\u003cn\u003e`\nconnection id, and shows an account only when every repository bound through\nthe connection has the same owner. The \"Create a new organization from it\"\nform is prefilled from that owner, otherwise empty. Two waiting connections\nfrom different accounts with no bound repositories look the same.\n\nDo\n\n- Store the account login on the connection when an `installation` /\n  `installation_repositories` delivery arrives (the delivery carries\n  `installation.account.login`), updating it on every delivery so a renamed\n  login shows its current name. Migration: a nullable column; existing rows\n  fill on the next delivery.\n- Add `account_login`, `account_id` and `installation_id` to `ConnectionDto`\n  (regenerate openapi.json and schema.ts).\n- UI: show the login and id in the panel row, prefill the new-org name from\n  the login, and drop the owner-of-bound-repos fallback.\n\nThe login is display only. The org-assignment check keeps using the immutable\naccount id (`delivery_assign_refusal`); a login can be renamed and taken by\nsomeone else.\n\nDone when a waiting GitHub connection with no bound repositories shows its\naccount login and id, and the create form is prefilled from the login.","files":null}]}